Gantry carries a debugging and automation surface so a site can be posed and
read from code without moving a real pointer or waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
game instance's `initialize` returns the finished surface. The engine holds it
and returns it from `engine.debug`, and it is reached that way alone: nothing
is installed on the page. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game
through it, so it is present and exactly as specified here. `src/game.ts`
declares and exports its type, `GantryDebugApi`.

Each operation is a reading or a pose acting on the live world at the moment of
the call. A pose sets one thing and leaves the rest of the game as it stands,
so a caller that wants several things arranged makes several calls, in the
order it wants them, and nothing it does not ask for happens. A pose arranges
the yard through the same systems play uses, so a posed edit lands in the
structure the placement rules govern, a posed tape step joins the tape the
program runs, and a run started through the surface is the run the `run` action
starts — and the rules those systems apply are what the RUN afterwards
exercises. A pose establishes a precondition and never an outcome; what happens
next comes from advancing the real simulation. Gantry runs in one world for the
whole session and every screen is a value of the screen field, so a pose that
changes the screen takes effect at the call rather than riding a level
transition.

The surface is not a player, and it never asks whether one could have reached
what it is doing. Which screen is showing, whether a run is in progress, which
tool is selected, and whether a control is drawn are how a player reaches an
effect; none of them is a condition on an operation, and every operation acts
from wherever the game stands. The one operation a run in progress figures in
is `abortRun`, where it is the act rather than a condition on reaching it: the
abort IS the ending of a run in progress, so with no run in progress there is
no abort to pose and the operations below say what it does then.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls
it.
{{/if}}

## A render-free core

The simulation is a fixed-tick core kept apart from its drawing. Three
properties make it driveable from code:

- Fixed timestep. Game state advances a tick at a time from the elapsed
  simulation time it is handed, and a tick is the same length at every frame
  rate and every watch speed.
- Render-free core. Game state advances from ticks and input alone,
  independent of the canvas, of the renderer, and of wall-clock time. The
  dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the renderer, and
  whether an edit is accepted is decided from the structure and the site alone.
- No refusal on the strength of the route. Every pose reaches the state it
  names, whatever screen is showing and whatever a player would have had to do
  to reach it, and a call naming nothing the game holds a state for throws
  rather than passing quietly. An edit rule is the one thing that still
  decides, because that rule is the edit and not a route to it: an edit the
  rule refuses leaves the yard exactly as it was, and a caller reads that from
  `snapshot`, as it reads what any pose left, rather than from a return value.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard, the pointer, the clock, and the overlay all belong to the runtime
layer beneath the game, and nothing outside this build owns any of them. The
surface is therefore what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `advance`, and what delivers a raw pointer or key act, through
the input operations below. It carries no operation for the registered actions
themselves: a caller presses the key `specs/controls.md` binds to an action and
the game reads the action exactly as it does for a player.

The camera and the projection that turns a world position into a point on the
stage are the runtime layer's too, so `project` below is the reading that
answers where a lattice node, a member end, or the hook is drawn.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, the pointer, the camera's projection, and the overlay
belong to the {{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/`
directory at the root of this repository, and the surface carries no operation
for any of them. The engine advances the game frame by frame and can take it
off real time, it reports the pointer position in the stage's logical units
along with the press and release edges, it delivers the actions the game
registers, it draws and toggles the debug overlay, and it projects a world
position to the point on the stage it is drawn at.

A caller reaching a control therefore dispatches a real pointer or key event at
the engine's surface and advances a frame, and the game handles it exactly as
it handles a player's. A caller that needs the stage point a lattice node, a
member end, or the hook is drawn at projects that world position through the
engine's camera.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Gantry's, under Diagnostics below.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`GANTRY_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number,
and the operations below. Every argument is a plain number, string, or boolean:
a world position is passed as its three coordinates in order, an angle is in
degrees, and an `id` is a member's identifier as `snapshot` reports it. Values
come back as plain numbers, strings, booleans, and plain objects.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Each operation takes only the parameters its table names, and an operation whose
name carries no parameter list takes none. A pose returns nothing; a reading
returns what it read and changes nothing.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-3d")}}
Each operation's first parameter is the current state,
`DeepReadonly<GantryState>`, before the parameters its table names, so an
operation whose name carries no parameter list takes the state alone. A pose
returns the next `GantryState`, built from the one it was handed, which it
leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read.
{{else}}
Each operation takes only the parameters its table names, and an operation whose
name carries no parameter list takes none. Each acts on the live world the
moment it is called. A pose returns nothing; a reading returns what it read and
changes nothing.
{{/if}}

Three rules cover every operation below.

- An argument outside the domain its operation states is invalid, and the call
  fails loudly rather than guessing what was meant. So is an index no site,
  load, or tape step carries, and an `id` no member carries. An operation whose
  table names a lattice node takes one: a coordinate that is not a multiple of
  `LATTICE_PITCH` is outside the domain and fails the same way, rather than
  being refused as an edit.
- A pose that stands for an EDIT — a member, the ring, a counterweight, a tape
  step, a run started — commits through the same rule the edit itself passes,
  because that rule is the edit and not a gate on reaching it: an over-budget
  member costs nothing and appears nowhere, a rate above an axis's maximum joins
  no step, and a run the structure is not ready for does not begin. Each of
  those outcomes is readable in the snapshot, and `check` says why a run would
  be refused.
- No operation asks which screen is showing, whether a run is in progress, or
  which tool is selected. Those are how a player reaches a control and are not
  an operation's conditions, so an operation acts from wherever the game stands:
  a structure pose edits the open site's structure with the program screen up,
  and a tape pose edits its tape with the build screen up. Nothing an operation
  is asked to do is passed over because of where the game stands; the edit
  rules of the bullet above are the only thing that refuses an edit. The one
  operation a run in progress figures in is `abortRun`, and it is not a gate
  there either: the abort IS the ending of a run in progress, so it is the act
  itself rather than a condition on reaching it, and where there is no run in
  progress there is no abort to pose. Its own row below says what it does then.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### The clock

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setAutoStep(enabled)` | Takes the game off real time and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the frame loop advancing the game from the wall clock, so it changes only when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to running itself, which is how a build starts and how it is played. Drawing is unaffected either way. |
| `advance(ticks)` | Runs `ticks` whole frames, immediately and in order, each covering `1 / TICK_HZ` seconds of elapsed time and each followed by a render. `ticks` defaults to `1`. |

A frame is a real frame, the same one the loop runs. During a run at watch speed
`1` a frame covers exactly one tick of the pipeline `specs/program.md` fixes, so
`advance(n)` runs `n` ticks. The watch speed scales what a frame covers exactly
as it scales a real frame, so a scenario counting ticks leaves the speed where a
run starts it. Off the run screen nothing ticks, and the frame is still real:
the input delivered since the last frame is read, the camera moves against that
elapsed time, and the scene is drawn.

Advancing while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the wall
clock is already doing, so a scenario that counts ticks calls
`setAutoStep(false)` first.

{{/if}}
### Readings

| Operation | Returns |
| --- | --- |
| `snapshot` | The plain object under Snapshot shape below. |
| `check` | The static check of `specs/structure.md`, computed on the spot. |

`check` reports exactly what the `check` action reports, in this shape:

```js
{
  issues: ["no-ring", ...],   // what would refuse a run, empty when nothing would
  cost: <number>,
  budget: <number>,
  stable: <boolean>,
  members: [{ id, force, utilization }],
}
```

`stable` is whether the structure stands and `members` is in member-id order.
`issues` carries each issue once, in the order `specs/structure.md` lists them:
`no-ring`, `no-rail`, `invalid-rail`, `disconnected-members`, and then
`empty-program` last. `specs/structure.md` fixes the issues, the solve the
check runs, and what a structure with an issue reports. The reading is pure: it
computes the check and returns it, and it displays nothing, so the result the
build screen is showing is untouched; `showCheck` below is the pose that shows
one.

| Operation | Returns |
| --- | --- |
| `drawn()` | What the last frame drew, as an array of the entries below, in no fixed order. |

Each entry is one thing the frame put on screen:

```js
{
  kind: "model",       // what sort of thing it is, from the table below
  name: "ring",        // which one, within its kind
  id: null,            // the member's id for a member, null otherwise
  source: "ring.glb",  // the produced file its geometry came from, or null
  x: 0, y: 6, z: 0,    // the world position it is drawn at
  yaw: 0,              // the yaw it is turned to, in degrees
  size: [2.5, 2, 2.5], // its extent in world units, as [width, height, depth]
  text: null,          // the string drawn, for `text`; null otherwise
}
```

| `kind` | `name` is | One entry per |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `model` | the model name `specs/assets.md` gives it | placement of that model |
| `member` | the member's material: `strut`, `cable`, or `rail` | intact member, carrying its `id` |
| `aid` | `lattice`, `envelope`, or `ramp-legend` | aid shown, whole rather than per cell |
| `mark` | `anchor`, `load-start`, `pad`, `pad-yaw`, `pending-node`, `picked-node`, or `selected-tool` | mark drawn |
| `obstacle` | `obstacle` | obstacle drawn |
| `cable` | `hoist` | hoist cable drawn |
| `ground` | `ground` | the yard floor, once |
| `text` | the build's own label for the readout it belongs to | run of text drawn, wherever it is drawn |

The reading describes the frame the game last drew and changes nothing: it is
taken after a frame rather than causing one, so a caller that has just advanced
reads what that advance drew. A thing the frame did not draw has no entry, which
is how the reading says a mark is absent, an aid is hidden, or a model was never
placed.

Two of those names are readouts rather than things in the yard, because
`specs/ui.md` fixes both by name: the build screen marks "the selected tool", and
the run screen carries "a legend for the utilization ramp". Where either is drawn
and what it looks like are the build's; that it is on screen is not.

`source` is the produced file the geometry was decoded from, named as
`specs/assets.md` names it and without any path or bundling suffix a build's
tooling adds, and it is `null` for anything the build drew in code. `size` is
what was actually drawn rather than what it stands for, so a model drawn at the
wrong scale reports the wrong size. A `text` entry's `name` is the build's own,
so what identifies a readout is the `text` it drew and not what the build calls
it.

`size` is an EXTENT and not a frame, and the `[width, height, depth]` above is
the extent of a thing standing square to the world. A thing drawn along a line
that leans — a member, the hoist cable — has no such box, and may report either
the box its two ends span or the cross-section and length it was drawn with;
nothing here chooses between them, and neither carries THE DIRECTION IT LEANS
IN, because `yaw` turns a thing about the vertical (`specs/world.md`) and says
nothing about a lean. What the two do agree on is the figure a reading of such
a thing needs, which is the extent's own length; and a bar drawn between two
points is drawn at the middle of them, so what an entry fixes about a thing
spanning two world positions is that length and that middle.

A `mark` standing for a LOAD POSE — `pad`, `pad-yaw`, `load-start` — carries
that pose's `x` and `z`, and a `y` anywhere between the surface the load rests
on and the lift point above it. The marking is drawn on the surface while the
pose it names is the lift point (`specs/world.md`), and a build may report
either, so what such an entry fixes is which surface the mark sits on rather
than its exact height.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The one exception is the debug overlay, which is not one of the game's readouts
at all: Diagnostics below fixes the `name` its lines are reported under.
{{/if}}

Art direction stays the build's throughout: the reading reports what a build
chose, and nothing here fixes a palette, a form, or a layout.

| Operation | Returns |
| --- | --- |
| `menuItemRect(index)` | The hit region of entry `index` of the menu the screen showing carries, as `{ x, y, w, h }`. |

`x` and `y` are the region's top-left corner in logical stage units and `w` and
`h` its size: the region a pointer selects that entry from and a contact takes
it in (`specs/ui.md`). It is a pure reading and moves nothing. Because
`specs/ui.md` leaves the menu layout to the build, this is how a caller finds
where an entry was drawn without knowing that layout, and it is what a caller
aims a press or a contact at. The three screens carrying a menu are `title`,
`select`, and `results`; called on any of the other four it fails loudly, and so
does an `index` the menu showing has no entry at.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}

| Operation | Returns |
| --- | --- |
| `project(x, y, z)` | The point on the stage the world position `(x, y, z)` is drawn at, through the camera as it stands, as `{ x, y, visible }`. |

`x` and `y` are logical stage units, and `visible` is whether the position lies
in front of the camera and inside the stage. It is a pure reading and moves
nothing. Because `specs/controls.md` fixes how a click picks rather than how the
yard is drawn, this is how a caller finds the point a node is drawn at without
knowing the projection: a press and release at a visible node's projected
point picks that node, and one further than `NODE_PICK_PX` from every node
picks none.
{{/if}}

### The run and the screens

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `reset` | Returns the game to its title state, as below. |
| `reconcile` | Brings every value the surface reports into agreement with the world as it now stands, without advancing anything, as below. |
| `setScreen(screen)` | Shows a named screen and sets nothing else; `menuIndex` is left as it stands (`specs/state.md`), so a caller posing a screen with a menu sets it with `setMenuIndex` afterwards. `screen` is one of the seven identifiers of `specs/ui.md`. |
| `setMenuIndex(index)` | Sets the highlighted entry of the menu on the screen showing, counted from `0`; the domain is the entry count of the menu that screen shows (`specs/ui.md`). |
| `openSite(index)` | Opens site `index`, counted from `0`, locked or not: the opening `specs/state.md` fixes, as below, and nothing else. The screen is left as it stands. |
| `setCleared(index, cleared)` | Sets whether site `index` has been cleared this session, and with it which sites are open. |
| `setBest(index, cost, time)` | Records `{ cost, time }` as site `index`'s best score, whatever it held. |
| `clearBest(index)` | Returns site `index` to having no recorded score. |
| `setCamera(yaw, pitch, dist)` | Sets the orbit camera's pose. The pitch and the distance limits `specs/controls.md` fixes are constants, so they are this operation's domain and a value outside either fails loudly; the yaw is unbounded and wraps to at or above `0` and below `360`. |
| `startRun` | Poses the `run` action: the same refusals, the same `run-start`, and the same move to the run screen as `specs/program.md` states. |
| `abortRun` | Poses the abort of a run IN PROGRESS, ending it with no verdict: `run` goes back to its idle placeholder and the build screen returns. With no run in progress there is no abort to pose, and nothing changes at all, as below. |
| `showCheck` | Poses the `check` action: computes the static check and leaves it showing, exactly as the action does (`specs/structure.md`), from whatever screen it is called on. |

`openSite` carries exactly the effects `specs/state.md` states for opening a
site and leaves the screen where it stands. It is the surface's way onto any
site without playing to it, and a site opened this way carries the loads and
obstacles `specs/sites.md` gives it, so a caller that wants a different set
poses it with the site operations below. Entering a site from the select screen
is the two operations `openSite` then `setScreen("build")`, in that order.

`setScreen` shows the screen and sets nothing else. Arriving at a screen in
play does more than show it: `specs/ui.md` puts the highlight somewhere on
every menu, entering `build` from the select screen opens a site, and a cleared
run records its score on the way to `results`. Each of those belongs to the
operation that carries it, so a caller that wants the arrival poses it as the
operations that make it up.

`startRun` and `abortRun` do move the screen, because each poses a player's ACT
rather than a state transition: `startRun` is the `run` action, with the
refusals `specs/program.md` gives it, `abortRun` is the abort, and each takes
the screen its act takes. `openSite` poses no act, only the opening
`specs/state.md` fixes, so it moves no screen.

Every one of these applies on every screen. `setMenuIndex` is the one with a
screen-dependent domain rather than a screen-dependent effect: the entry count
it is checked against is the menu the screen showing carries, so on one of the
four screens carrying no menu — `howto`, `build`, `program`, and `run` — there
is no entry to name and the call fails loudly. `startRun` still carries the
`run` action's own refusals, which are the structure's readiness and not a
screen: it is refused exactly as the action is, leaving the state as it was, and
`check` says why it would refuse. `abortRun` WITH NO RUN IN PROGRESS HAS NO
ABORT TO POSE AND DOES NOTHING AT ALL: a run already idle stays idle, a run that
has ended keeps its phase, its cause, its clock and its broken list, and the
screen stays exactly where it was. The build screen the row above returns to is
the screen the abort itself takes, and where there is no abort there is no
screen to take — so an ended run read on the run screen is still read there
afterwards. `showCheck` computes the check and leaves it showing wherever it is
called. A run that begins is the ordinary run, and it
starts as `specs/state.md` says a run starts: nothing has ticked at the call, so `run.tick` reads `0` immediately
after it, and the axes, the pivot, and the bob stand at the run-start values
that file gives.

`reconcile` brings every value this surface reports into agreement with the
world as it now stands, without advancing anything. The structure's `cost` and
`issues` and the `pick` a click at the pointer would take all follow from the
structure, the site and the pointer, so after a pose they answer for the yard as
it now is rather than as it was before. A build
that works those out at the read has nothing to do here and this call changes
nothing; a build that keeps any of them as a stored copy rewrites that copy from
what it is a copy of.

It moves no clock and runs no tick: `simTime` and `run.tick` are untouched, no
axis advances, no step is taken, no pendulum is stepped, no collision is tested,
and no solve is run for its own sake. It plays no cue, shows no check result,
changes no screen, pushes no history, and reaches no verdict. It moves nothing
to make a reading agree: a bob posed away from its cable stays where it was put,
and a value posed past a limit is not brought back inside it. What a tick owns
stays the tick's, so `run.forces` is the latest solve's and `run.pivot` is still
the tick's to move, and `historyDepth`, `best` and `run.broken` are untouched. Calling it twice leaves the same state as
calling it once. It is never an error and may be called on any screen, whether
or not a run is in progress.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-3d")}}

`reconcile(state)` returns the next `GantryState`, built from the one it was
handed, which it leaves as it was. A build that works every one of those
readings out at the call returns a state equal to the one it was given.
{{/if}}

`reset` restores every field the snapshot reports to its title-screen value, bar
one: the `title` screen with `menuIndex` `0`, site `0` open, every site
uncleared with no recorded score, every site's stored structure and tape
emptied, the open site's loads and obstacles back to the ones `specs/sites.md`
gives it, the strut tool, no pending node, an empty history, the camera at its
start pose, no check result showing, an idle run, and `simTime` at `0`. The one
field left alone is `muted`, the game's readable copy of a mute bit it does not
own (`specs/state.md`), which `reset` leaves as it stands rather than turning a
player's preference off. Because `reset` restores exactly those fields and the
six the table below gives, anything else the build keeps across frames is
derived from them.

The pointer is six fields, and this table is the whole of what sets each:

| Field | On a `reset` | What else sets it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `x` | `0` | Every update reads the pointer's current position into it (`specs/controls.md`), so a `reset` shows in it only until the next update. |
| `y` | `0` | The same, on the other axis. |
| `down` | `false` | A press sets it `true` and a release sets it `false`, and no update rewrites it in between: it is the game's own record of the press it is following rather than a reading of the button taken afresh. After a `reset` it is `false` until the next press. |
| `pressX` | `0` | A press writes the position it went down at, and a release leaves it, so it rests where the last press began. |
| `pressY` | `0` | The same, on the other axis. |
| `dragging` | `false` | The move on which a live press reaches `CLICK_SLOP` (`6`) from where it went down sets it `true`, and the release sets it `false` (`specs/controls.md`). |

### The structure

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `clearStructure` | Empties the open site's structure: every member, the ring, and every counterweight go, and the undo history is pushed exactly as one edit pushes it. |
| `addMember(ax, ay, az, bx, by, bz, material)` | Places a member between the two lattice nodes, of `"strut"`, `"cable"`, or `"rail"`. |
| `removeMember(id)` | Removes the member carrying that id. |
| `setRing(x, y, z)` | Places the slew ring by its base corner at that lattice node. |
| `clearRing` | Removes the ring. |
| `addCounterweight(x, y, z)` | Places a counterweight on that lattice node. |
| `removeCounterweight(x, y, z)` | Removes the counterweight on that lattice node. |
| `setTool(tool)` | Selects a build tool, as the tool actions do. |
| `setPendingNode(x, y, z)` | Holds that lattice node as the pending first node of a member placement, as a first click does. |
| `clearPendingNode` | Clears the pending node without placing, as `back` does. |

These pose single edits on the open site's structure, entering the rule pipeline
the build tools feed, so every refusal in `specs/structure.md` applies unchanged
and an edit that rule refuses leaves the structure exactly as it was. That rule
is the edit rather than a gate on reaching it, which is why it stays; the build
screen is how a player reaches the tools and is not a condition here, so these
edit the open site's structure whatever screen is showing. Each edit that lands
pushes the undo history exactly as a click would. `addMember` gives the member
the structure's `nextMemberId` and advances it by one.

A removal with nothing to remove takes the structure to the state it names,
which it is already in: `clearRing` on a crane carrying no ring and
`removeCounterweight` on a lattice node carrying none both leave the structure
exactly as it stands and push no history.

`clearStructure` empties the structure as opening a site with nothing built
leaves it, so the next member placed after it takes id `0` and a posed crane's
ids run upward in the order its members were added. It is refused by nothing:
removal is always allowed. On a structure that is already empty it removes
nothing and pushes no history, like every other removal with nothing to remove,
and `nextMemberId` goes back to `0` whether or not anything went.

A caller that wants a whole crane poses it as the sequence of edits that builds
it, which is what puts it under the same rules a player builds under.

### The tape

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `clearProgram` | Empties the open site's tape. |
| `addMoveStep(axis, target, rate)` | Appends a move step carrying one command: drive `axis` to `target` at `rate`. |
| `addCommand(index, axis, target, rate)` | Adds a command to the move step at `index`. |
| `addActionStep(action)` | Appends an action step, `"attach"` or `"release"`. |
| `removeStep(index)` | Removes the step at `index`. |

An `axis` is `"slew"`, `"trolley"`, `"hoist"`, or `"grip"`. These pose tape
edits on the open site's tape, wherever the game stands — the program screen is
how a player reaches the tape editor (`specs/controls.md`) and is not a
condition on these — and each passes the rules that editor applies
(`specs/program.md`): a rate at most the axis's max rate and greater than `0`,
and at most one command per axis in a step. A command on an axis its step
already commands is refused, as is a command added to an action step. Targets
are accepted as written and judged when the step starts.

### The site

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `clearLoads` | Removes every load from the open site. |
| `addLoad(cls, mass, x, y, z, yaw)` | Appends a load of class `"crate"`, `"container"`, or `"drum"`, of that mass, starting at rest with its lift point at `(x, y, z)` and that yaw. Its target pose starts equal to its starting pose. |
| `setLoadTarget(index, x, y, z, yaw)` | Sets the target pose of the load at `index`, which is the pad it must be set down on. |
| `clearObstacles` | Removes every obstacle from the open site. |
| `addObstacle(x, y, z, w, h, d)` | Appends an obstacle: the axis-aligned box with minimum corner `(x, y, z)` and size `(w, h, d)`. |

The site poses apply wherever the game stands, run or no run: the yard is the
open site's and the screen is the player's route to it. `openSite` leaves the
screen as it stands, so a caller that wants a screen with the site it opened
sets the screen itself. They are what lets a scenario hold only what it is
about: a caller clears the
loads and the obstacles and adds back exactly the ones its requirement
concerns, so a check on a lift runs against one load and a check on a member
striking a wall runs against one obstacle. The loads a run carries are the ones
standing when it starts, index-aligned with them, and `openSite` and `reset`
both put the site's authored set back.

Loads and obstacles are the site's, not the structure's, so posing them refuses
nothing and changes nothing that is built: a member standing where an obstacle
is posed stays where it is, and the run that follows ends as
`structure-struck-obstacle` on the tick the collision test reaches it. The
editor's refusals govern edits to the structure, and posing the site is not one
of those.

The loads and the obstacles are the whole of what a site puts in the yard and
the whole of what these operations reach. A site's name, envelope, anchors,
budget, and par are the site's own figures, read by the open site's index
against `specs/sites.md` rather than carried anywhere, so no operation sets one
and `openSite` is what chooses between them: the six sites carry six
envelopes, six anchor sets, and six budgets, and a scenario about an envelope,
an anchor set, or a budget opens the site carrying the one it is about.

### The run in progress

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setAxis(axis, value)` | Sets an axis's value, leaving it stopped with no live command. |
| `setAxisRate(axis, rate)` | Sets an axis's signed rate, leaving its value and its command as they are. |
| `setBob(x, y, z)` | Puts the pendulum bob at a world position. |
| `setBobVelocity(vx, vy, vz)` | Sets the bob's velocity. |
| `setLoadPose(index, x, y, z, yaw)` | Puts a load's lift point at a world position, at that yaw. |
| `setLoadPhase(index, phase)` | Sets a load's phase to `"waiting"`, `"attached"`, `"placed"`, or `"lost"`. |
| `setRunTick(tick)` | Sets the number of ticks the run has taken, from which the run clock is `tick / TICK_HZ` seconds (`specs/state.md`). The domain is the whole numbers at or above `0`. |
| `setSpeedIndex(index)` | Sets the watch speed to that index into `RUN_SPEEDS`. |

The first seven pose the run the snapshot reports, which is the run in
progress where there is one and the idle placeholder where there is not;
whether a run is live is not a condition on them. `setSpeedIndex` poses the
watch speed, as the `speed` action does, wherever the game stands and whether or
not a run that screen is showing has ended. Each sets what it names and leaves
the rest of the run as it stands, and none of them reaches a verdict: the run's
own rules decide clearing, breakage, and every failure on the ticks that follow.

`setLoadPhase` to `"attached"` hangs that load on the hook exactly as a
successful `attach` leaves it, without the candidate search and without the
`attach-missed` verdict. The hook holds one load, which is the rigging's own
rule rather than a gate on reaching the control, so a second load asked onto an
occupied hook is not hung. The
grip's value is left as it is, so a caller that wants the hook square on the
load sets the grip too. Every other phase takes the load off the hook, so the
run's attachment clears when the load posed was the one hanging. `"placed"`
sets the load down exactly as a successful `release` leaves it: it sits at
exactly its target pose and stays there for the rest of the run
(`specs/world.md`), and `"waiting"` and `"lost"` leave the load at the pose it
holds when it comes off.

`setRunTick` poses how long the run has been going and nothing else. The tape's
position, the axes, the loads, and the watch speed stand where they were, the
run clock the snapshot reports and the score a clear records both follow the
number posed, and the run's next tick is numbered one above it.

`setAxis` takes any value the axis can hold. The ranges in `specs/program.md`
are what a step's target is judged against when the step starts, not a bound on
the axis itself.

`setAxisRate` sets the rate the snapshot reports at once, and poses a
precondition like every other pose rather than an outcome: an axis carrying no
live command holds its value with zero rate (`specs/program.md`), so the next
tick of a run leaves a rate posed onto such an axis at `0` again. Posing a rate
onto an axis that is under a command sets what that axis is doing as the
controller next reads it.

`setBob` puts the bob where it is asked for, so a caller that wants a bob the
cable can hold sets the hoist axis to the distance it left between the pivot and
the bob. The pendulum's own constraint runs on the next tick either way.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### Input

These feed the same input path the runtime layer feeds, so a posed press and a
player's press are the same event to the game. Nothing is bypassed: the click
and orbit rules, the pick radii, the tool behavior, and the screen restrictions
all run exactly as they do for a player. Beyond the pointer position
`pointerMove` moves, they set no state of their own: what each changes is
whatever the act it delivers changes.

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `pointerMove(x, y)` | Moves the pointer to a logical stage position. |
| `pointerDown(x, y)` | A press at a logical stage position. It moves the pointer there first, so a press needs no `pointerMove` before it. |
| `pointerUp` | A release at the position the pointer is at. |
| `touchDown(x, y)` | A touch contact landing at a logical stage position. |
| `touchUp` | The contact lifting, at the position it landed at. |
| `keyDown(code)` | Presses a key down. `code` is a standard `KeyboardEvent.code`. A one-shot action it fires on the current screen applies, and a held action stays held until `keyUp`. |
| `keyUp(code)` | Releases a key. |

Each delivers one act, and a caller that wants a gesture made of several
delivers them in order: a click is a `pointerDown` followed by a `pointerUp`,
an orbit drag is a `pointerDown`, one or more `pointerMove` calls, and a
`pointerUp`, and a tap is a `touchDown` followed by a `touchUp`. The contact the
touch pair delivers is a contact and not the pointer: it moves no pointer
position and raises no press or release of one, so a build that tells the two
apart is delivered exactly what `specs/controls.md` says its runtime reports.

A build is free to act on an event as it arrives or on the frame that reads it,
so a caller that needs the game to have consumed one runs a frame after it.

{{/if}}
## Snapshot shape

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-3d")}}
`snapshot` returns:
{{else}}
`snapshot()` returns:
{{/if}}

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "select" | "build" | "program" | "run" | "results",
  menuIndex: <number>,             // the highlighted menu entry, from 0
  siteIndex: <number>,             // the open, or last open, site, from 0
  cleared: [<boolean>],            // one per site
  best: [{ cost, time } | null],   // one per site
  site: {                          // the open site, as it currently stands
    name: <string>,
    envelope: { min: { x, y, z }, max: { x, y, z } },
    anchors: [{ x, y, z }],
    budget: <number>,
    par: { cost: <number>, time: <number> },
    loads: [
      {
        class: "crate" | "container" | "drum",
        mass: <number>,
        from: { x, y, z, yaw },   // the lift point it starts at, and its yaw
        to:   { x, y, z, yaw },   // the pad it is wanted on
      },
    ],
    obstacles: [{ min: { x, y, z }, size: { x, y, z } }],
  },
  tool: "strut" | "cable" | "rail" | "ring" | "counterweight" | "delete",
  pendingNode: { x, y, z } | null,
  historyDepth: <number>,          // edits the open site can still undo
  camera: { yaw, pitch, dist },
  pointer: {
    x: <number>, y: <number>,      // logical stage units
    down: <boolean>,               // a press is live
    pressX: <number>,              // where the live press went down
    pressY: <number>,
    dragging: <boolean>,           // the press has reached CLICK_SLOP
  },
  pick: {                          // what a click at the pointer would take
    node: { x, y, z } | null,
    member: <id> | null,
  },
  structure: {
    members: [{ id, a: { x, y, z }, b: { x, y, z }, material }],
    nextMemberId: <number>,        // the id the next member takes
    ring: { corner: { x, y, z } } | null,
    counterweights: [{ x, y, z }],
    cost: <number>,
    issues: [<string>],            // the readiness issues, empty when ready
  },
  program: [
    { kind: "move", commands: [{ axis, target, rate }] },
    { kind: "action", action: "attach" | "release" },
  ],
  checkResult: {                   // what the build screen is showing, or null
    issues: [<string>],
    cost: <number>,
    budget: <number>,
    stable: <boolean>,
    members: [{ id, force, utilization }],
  } | null,
  run: {
    phase: "idle" | "running" | "cleared" | "failed",
    cause: <string> | null,
    tick: <number>,
    time: <number>,                // tick / TICK_HZ, seconds
    speedIndex: <number>,          // into RUN_SPEEDS
    stepIndex: <number>,
    stepLive: <boolean>,           // that step is taken and not yet complete
    axes: {
      slew:    { value, rate, command: { target, rate } | null },
      trolley: { value, rate, command: { target, rate } | null },
      hoist:   { value, rate, command: { target, rate } | null },
      grip:    { value, rate, command: { target, rate } | null },
    },
    pivot: { x, y, z },            // the point the cable hangs from
    bob: { pos: { x, y, z }, vel: { x, y, z } },
    attached: <number> | null,     // the attached load's index
    loads: [{ phase, pos: { x, y, z }, yaw }],
    forces: [{ id, force, utilization }],
    broken: [<number>],            // member ids, in the order they broke
  },
  muted: <boolean>,
  simTime: <number>,
}
```

The shape is fixed and every field is present whatever the screen. `site`,
`structure`, and `program` report the open site's, and the structure and tape
authored on another site are read by opening that site. `run.loads` carries one
entry per load the run started with, in that order (`specs/state.md`); the idle
placeholder carries no load entries. A field the current screen does not use
reports the value it is holding rather than going missing, and the table below
gives the value each rests at.

| Field | Resting value |
| --- | --- |
| `menuIndex` | `0` before a menu has been moved, and on a screen with no menu — `howto`, `build`, `program`, and `run` — what the last menu screen left it holding |
| `pendingNode` | `null` until a first node is held, and again once it is placed or cleared |
| `historyDepth` | `0` on a site opened and not yet edited |
| `pointer` | `{ x: 0, y: 0, down: false, pressX: 0, pressY: 0, dragging: false }` before the pointer has been used; after that, what the pointer table under `reset` above gives each field |
| `pick` | `{ node: null, member: null }` on every screen but `build`, and where nothing is in range |
| `checkResult` | `null` while the build screen is showing no check result |
| `run` | the idle placeholder `specs/state.md` gives it before a site's first run, and again whenever a site is opened, a run IN PROGRESS is aborted, or `reset` is called; otherwise the run last started, left as it ended until the next one starts |

- `site.name`, `site.envelope`, `site.anchors`, `site.budget`, and `site.par`
  are the open site's own figures, read by `siteIndex` against
  `specs/sites.md`; `site.loads` and `site.obstacles` are the yard as it stands.
- `structure.cost` and `structure.issues` are derived from the rules in
  `specs/structure.md`, so `issues` is empty exactly when the structure is
  ready. `structure.issues` and `checkResult.issues` are in the order `check`
  reports them.
- `structure.nextMemberId` is the id the next member placed takes, which
  `clearStructure` returns to `0` and which no removal gives back.
- `pick` is the node candidate and the member candidate a click at the pointer's
  current position would take, decided by the pick radii and the tie-breaks
  `specs/controls.md` fixes. Either is `null` when nothing is in range.
- `pointer.pressX` and `pointer.pressY` are in the same logical stage units as
  `pointer.x` and `pointer.y`. The pointer table under `reset` above is what
  sets each of the six fields, and `specs/controls.md` fixes what separates a
  click from an orbit drag.
- `checkResult` is the check result currently standing, exactly as the `check`
  action or the `showCheck` pose left it, and it is what the build screen draws.
  The `check` reading never sets it.
- `run.stepLive` is whether the step at `run.stepIndex` has been taken and is
  not yet complete, and `run.pivot` is the point the cable hangs from at the
  most recent tick's geometry, which the pendulum reads on the tick after
  (`specs/rigging.md`); from a run's start until its first tick it is the
  run-start pivot `specs/state.md` gives, and the idle placeholder's is the
  zero vector that file states.
- `run.forces` is the latest solve's, in member-id order, and covers the
  members still intact. It is empty until a run's first solve.
- `muted` is the game's readable copy of the mute bit `specs/state.md`
  declares, and `simTime` accumulates every update's delta time whatever the
  screen.

Every field is read straight off the game's own state or derived from it, so
what the snapshot reports is what the game holds. A build is free to work any
derived field out at the read — `structure.cost` and `structure.issues`, and
`pick`, are the ones that follow from the world as it stands — or to keep it as
a stored copy, and `reconcile` is what brings a stored copy back into agreement
after a pose. `run.forces` is not one of them: it is the latest solve's reading
rather than a reading of the structure as it now stands, so a pose does not
change it and the tick does. Every field an
operation above sets is reported here, so a call is checked by setting a value
and reading it back. Two are read a step apart. The undo stack is reported as
`historyDepth`, and each entry comes back through `structure` as an `undo`
restores it. A site that is not open reports the structure and tape stored on it
through `structure` and `program` once it is opened.

## Diagnostics

The debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as diagnostic
sources. Register at least the current screen and site, the structure's member
count, cost, and readiness issue count, the run's phase, step index, clock, and
cause, each axis's value, the bob's position, the highest current utilization
and the broken-member count, and the camera pose. Keep each one short enough to
read on a line, and keep every source a pure read, so watching the overlay
leaves the game as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, it is shown and hidden by the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code`
`Backquote`), it is off until toggled, and it reads the game without changing
it. Keep it visually plain and clearly separate from the game's own display.

Each line the overlay draws while it is shown comes back through `drawn()` as a
`text` entry named `overlay`, carrying that line as its `text`, so a caller
reads the panel without reading pixels. It is the one `text` entry whose `name`
this file fixes; every other one is the build's own.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-3d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Gantry's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the read,
as `DeepReadonly<GantryState>`, and reads off that argument. Drawing the panel,
showing and hiding it with the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`),
and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Gantry's part, through the world's
diagnostics in the game mode's `beginPlay`. Each source is a function of no
arguments, called at each read; read the live state at the call, through the
world the mode holds, so the panel reports the frame being drawn. Drawing the
panel and showing and hiding it with the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code`
`Backquote`) are the engine's.
{{/if}}
